cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  Measuring the Quantum Signature of Relational Time on
  Superconducting Hardware
message: >-
  If you use this software, data, or the experimental protocols
  in this repository, please cite it as below.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: "O'Neill"
    given-names: "Kevin"
    # orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0000-0000-0000"   # add before release
repository-code: "https://github.com/IllI/relational-time-ibm-quantum"
doi: 10.5281/zenodo.21878786
abstract: >-
  An experimental program testing whether relational time -- time defined by
  correlations between a clock and the rest of a system rather than by an
  external parameter -- leaves a measurable quantum signature distinguishable
  from its classical mimic. Four pre-registered runs on IBM Quantum Heron
  processors establish, in order: that the conditional-evolution signal
  usually cited as "time from entanglement" is reproduced by a classical
  clock control; that the clock-marginal coherence witness is not, and has a
  measured decoherence threshold past which apparent temporal dynamics
  survives its own quantum signature; that this witness is nonetheless
  necessary but not sufficient, since a zero-entanglement coherent product
  state scores higher on it; that no functional of a single measurement
  setting can certify clock-system entanglement (demonstrated adversarially,
  then proved); and finally that a multi-setting fidelity witness does
  certify it (F = 0.94/0.88 against a derived bound of 0.5), with both
  adversarial states correctly rejected. Includes the preceding
  cross-datacenter TPU telemetry nulls and the synthetic Page-Wootters /
  D-LinOSS simulation campaign from which the hypothesis and the
  measurement-diversity certification standard originate. All raw counts,
  pre-registrations, and server-side provenance are archived; every analysis
  reproduces from counts.
keywords:
  - quantum information
  - Page-Wootters mechanism
  - relational time
  - emergent time
  - quantum reference frames
  - entanglement witness
  - decoherence
  - IBM Quantum
  - superconducting qubits
  - pre-registration
  - state-space models
  - D-LinOSS
